    "text": "The dispatch office smelled of burnt coffee and the low, constant hum of the server rack. It was a smell Elias had long stopped noticing, just as he\u2019d stopped noticing the way the fluorescent lights buzzed overhead or the way the linoleum floor was worn thin in a path from his desk to the coffee pot. The hours between 2 and 4 a.m. were a country of their own, a quiet, grey land populated only by the blinking red and green lights on his board and the ghostly schedules of freight trains.\n\nThe 3:15 to Fulton was running late, a common enough occurrence. While he waited for the signal from the yard, he pulled a thick, dog-eared book from the shelf behind him. It was a relic, a bound volume of timetables from the old A&P line, decommissioned before he was even born. He liked the weight of it, the smell of old paper and glue. It was a tangible link to the history of the very tracks he now managed from a glowing screen.\n\nHe opened it at random, the spine cracking softly. The page was for a route that no longer existed, a spur line to a town that was now just a name on a map. As his finger traced the long-gone schedule, a small, folded piece of paper, yellowed with age, slipped from the binding and fluttered to his desk.\n\nIt wasn't a printed form. It was a note, written in a careful, looping cursive that spoke of a different era. The ink was a faded blue.\n\n*J",
